    "narrative": "For the August 24 slate, **Corbin Carroll** (74.65) and **Seiya Suzuki** (72.23) stand out as our elite anchors. **Corbin Carroll** is a priority start, leveraging a prime #2 spot in the order against Kevin Gausman; the 86% stolen base efficiency against Gausman\u2019s methodical delivery makes him a category-winning threat. **Seiya Suzuki** projects as our strongest overall play despite the \"Lineup Pending\" flag. Facing Merrill Kelly\u2014who sports a shaky 5.27 xERA\u2014provides a massive skill-based upside that offsets his neutral platoon dynamic, making him an essential component of today\u2019s output.\n\nIn the middle tier, **Mickey Moniak** (60.19) and **Max Muncy** (59.80) are positioned for high-efficiency production. **Mickey Moniak** benefits from a 14% boost due to favorable platoon splits against the right-handed Cavalli, while **Max Muncy** gains significant value from a park factor lift and a high-SB-probability matchup against Matthews. Conversely, **Josh Naylor** (47.91) and **Zach McKinstry** (40.85) remain in the lineup but carry elevated risk; **Josh Naylor** is fighting through a difficult matchup against Zack Wheeler, and **Zach McKinstry** barely clears our 40-point floor threshold, salvaged only by favorable wind conditions that might help drive the ball out.\n\nOur bench decision came down to **Cole Young**, who fell short at 31.76. While his base projection is serviceable, the combination of facing an elite arm in Wheeler and the defensive suppression caused by an elite catcher (1.86s pop time) tipped the optimizer to keep him on the bench. We are managing the bench tightly to preserve our remaining game caps, and with **James Wood** still sidelined on the IL, we are leaning on the efficiency of our daily starters rather than risking marginal bench production. We remain in a holding pattern for **Jose Caballero** and **Brice Turang** due to their teams' respective off-days, ensuring we prioritize active, high-floor matchups to climb the Roto standings.",
